                    <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-6">Overview</h2>
                    <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed text-lg">
                        This project involves the design, fabrication, and control of a cable-driven robotic arm biologically inspired by octopus tentacles. Unlike traditional rigid robots that use discrete joints, this arm utilizes a continuous backbone to achieve a smooth curling motion.
                    </p>
                    <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed text-lg mt-4">
                        The goal was to create a low-cost, modular platform capable of high-articulation grasping using FDM 3D printing and hobbyist electronics. By leveraging logarithmic spiral geometry, the arm can curl tightly onto itself or wrap around objects of varying sizes, mimicking the biological versatility of a tentacle.
                    </p>
                </section>

                <!-- Section: Mechanical Design -->
                <section id="design" class="bg-slate-50 p-8 rounded-2xl">
                    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-6">Mechanical Design & Geometry</h2>
                    
                    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-8 mb-8">
                        <div>
                            <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-800 mb-3">Logarithmic Spiral</h3>
                            <p class="text-slate-600 mb-4">
                                The arm's geometry is derived from a logarithmic spiral equation to ensure constant grasping angles regardless of object size:
                            </p>
                            <div class="bg-white p-4 rounded-lg shadow-sm text-center mb-4">
                                \[ \rho = ae^{\beta\theta} \]
                            </div>
                            <p class="text-slate-600">
                                Where \(\rho\) is the radius, \(\theta\) is the angle, and \(\beta\) controls the growth rate. The arm consists of discrete units scaled down by a factor of \(\beta=0.94\) for each subsequent unit. This taper ensures the arm curls from the tip (lowest stiffness) to the base.
                            </p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="h-64 bg-white rounded-xl border-2 border-dashed border-slate-300 flex items-center justify-center text-slate-400 text-center p-4">
                            <span>Figure 2: Dimensioned drawings of the base hexagonal unit</span>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-8">
                         <div class="order-2 md:order-1 h-64 bg-white rounded-xl border-2 border-dashed border-slate-300 flex items-center justify-center text-slate-400 text-center p-4">
                            <span>Figure 1: Isometric view of hexagonal unit</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="order-1 md:order-2">
                            <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-800 mb-3">Torsion Control</h3>
                            <p class="text-slate-600">
                                To prevent unwanted twisting during actuation, each unit was designed with hexagonal interlocking keys (2 notches and 2 grooves). This mechanical constraint counters torsional forces, keeping all units aligned while allowing the necessary 50° total bending angle per segment.
                            </p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <!-- Section: Electronics & Control -->
                <section id="electronics">
